          "choice": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "A solar eclipse occurs when the Moon passes between the Earth and the Sun, temporarily blocking the Sun's light either partially or fully. This alignment causes a shadow to fall on parts of the Earth, and it can only happen during a new moon phase. There are three main types of solar eclipses: total, partial, and annular, depending on the alignment and distances between the Earth, Moon, and Sun. Eclipses are observable from limited areas on Earth, with total eclipses offering the most dramatic view when the sun is entirely obscured."
            }
